---
/**
 * foodcraft / GalleryBL — "Kalinaw Coffee"'s photo bento.
 *
 * ROLE. The one section on the page that is nothing but the owner's own
 * photographs: a rust eyebrow, one display headline carrying the template's
 * italic rust emphasis, and the design's five-slot mosaic — a tall lead print
 * spanning both rows beside four smaller frames on cream.
 *
 * THE DESIGN IS THE SPECIFICATION. Every metric below is lifted verbatim from
 * the element carrying data-component="GALLERY" in
 * "Kalinaw Coffee Landing.dc.html":
 *
 *   section padding    92px top; 96px bottom — the design gives EVERY section a
 *                      bare bottom and lets the NEXT one own the gap it wants
 *                      (HOW-IT-WORKS asks for `margin-top:96px`, TESTIMONIALS
 *                      for `padding:96px … 0`). In THIS PORT the section that
 *                      follows declines that margin, so the 96px is carried
 *                      here instead — read the decision note below before
 *                      changing either number.
 *   eyebrow            10px / .42em caps, #B4541F              → .kick
 *   headline           Fraunces 400, clamp(31px,4vw,54px), lh 1.08,
 *                      margin `.26em 0 34px`, #241A12, and — alone among this
 *                      design's headlines — NO max-width
 *   emphasis           `Inside <em>…</em>`, italic #B4541F     → .h2 em
 *   grid               minmax(0,2fr) minmax(0,1fr) minmax(0,1fr)
 *   rows               186px 186px            gap 13px
 *   tile               #F2E8D8 ground, 14px radius, clipped    → .plate
 *   lead tile          grid-row: span 2
 *
 * NO LITERAL HEX APPEARS IN THIS FILE. Every colour the design names already
 * has a wrapper token with the same value: #B4541F is --accent, #241A12 is
 * --ink, #F2E8D8 is --well, 14px is --r-md. The tile ground, radius and clip
 * all arrive with .plate, so this file states geometry only.
 *
 * ── EDITABLE HOOKS (each one DECLARED in components/editor/genericContentSchema.ts)
 *   gallery.tag                        → the rust eyebrow (.kick)
 *   gallery.headline                   → the display h2, set:html
 *   gallery.items.<original i>.image   → data-image-field, the bento tiles
 *   gallery.items.<original i>.caption → the optional espresso caption pill
 * Nothing else. A data-field on any other path is a dead hook no sidebar input
 * owns, so no other path is written here.
 *
 * ── NOT SUPPLIED BY THIS COMPONENT ──────────────────────────────────────────
 * NOT ONE VISIBLE WORD. The design's slot placeholders — "The room",
 * "Pour-over bar", "Pastry case", "Brunch plate", "Beans / roaster" — are the
 * DESIGNER filling their own template. They are captions for photographs
 * nobody has looked at, so they are not hard-coded and no caption default is
 * invented: an uncaptioned photo is simply a photo. The same goes for the
 * headline; the design's own "Inside <em>Kalinaw Coffee</em>" is the
 * designer's copy, and an owner who typed no headline gets no headline
 * ELEMENT — every guard sits outside its tag, so there is never a rust eyebrow
 * hanging over nothing and never an empty caption pill on a photo.
 *
 * ── THE SECTION GATE ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 * The section renders only when at least one row carries an IMAGE. It counts
 * the BODY, never the tag or the headline: lib/derive-content-defaults.ts sets
 * gallery.tag ('Inside') and gallery.headline ('A look at our work') on EVERY
 * site, so a gate that counted them would ship an eyebrow and a headline over
 * an empty grid. Once photos exist the tag and headline render (still guarded)
 * — refusing them would leave two live sidebar inputs wired to nothing.
 *
 * ── DERIVED, not authored ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
 * ROW INDEXES. Rows without an image are dropped, and the ORIGINAL array index
 * travels with the survivor as `.i`. The editor writes the data-field path
 * verbatim into the draft, so a re-indexed path would patch a different row:
 * clearing row 2's photo would misdirect row 3's caption edits into row 2.
 * Nothing decorative is numbered here, so no display index is needed.
 *
 * THE COUNT LADDER. The design hard-codes five slots; content does not —
 * derive-content-defaults hands over up to six photos, and an owner may upload
 * one or eleven. So the design's five-up is the TOP of a ladder, each tier
 * drawn for its own count so none can open a hole in the mosaic:
 *   1  one full-width print, still two rows tall
 *   2  two full-height columns on the design's lopsided 2fr / 1fr
 *   3  the tall lead plus two stacked beside it
 *   4  the design's three tracks, the fourth frame taking both narrow ones
 *   5  the design's own grid, exactly
 *   6+ extras flow into implicit 186px rows and the LAST frame stretches over
 *      whatever tracks its row left empty (.fill-*), so the mosaic never ends
 *      on a ragged half-row. The stretch is computed per breakpoint — a
 *      three-track row and a two-track row run out at different counts — and
 *      is reset entirely at one column.
 *
 * ── DECISIONS A READER MIGHT OTHERWISE THINK WERE OVERSIGHTS ────────────────
 * · THE 96px BELOW THIS SECTION IS ITS OWN PADDING, not the next section's
 *   margin. The design writes that gap on HOW-IT-WORKS (`margin-top:96px`),
 *   which is where a literal port would put it — but HowBL deliberately carries
 *   NO top margin (its own frontmatter says so: PageBL declares a symmetric
 *   shared `section` rhythm, and it expects the PRECEDING section's bottom
 *   padding to be the gap), and every other cream BL section pads
 *   symmetrically. A zero bottom here therefore inherits a gap from nowhere: it
 *   butts the photo mosaic flush against the espresso band, with no cream
 *   between them. So this section carries the 96px the band asks for, written
 *   in exactly ONE place. Do not zero it here, and do not give HowBL a top
 *   margin as well — THAT is what would double it. (Hero → Trust is this
 *   family's one coordinated bare-bottom pair: HeroBL ends `58px 0 0` and
 *   TrustBL opens `62px 0 0`, so the design's 62px is drawn once.)
 * · NO onPage() ANCHOR-REMAP HELPER. This section renders no link, no button
 *   and no numeral: it declares no data-href-field and emits no href at all,
 *   so the helper its sibling sections carry would have nothing to remap. It
 *   is omitted rather than carried dead. The section's own id="gallery" is one
 *   of the page's five anchors and is what the header links AT.
 * · The design's 34px between the head and the mosaic sits on the HEAD BLOCK,
 *   not on the h2's own margin-bottom. With both strings present it renders
 *   identically (the h2 keeps the design's .26em above and adds nothing
 *   below); with the headline cleared, an eyebrow-only head still clears the
 *   photos instead of gluing them to its underside.
 * · .h2 max-width is released to none. The wrapper caps every headline at
 *   24ch; this design's gallery headline is the one it does NOT cap (its
 *   About, Why, How and CTA headlines all carry an explicit max-width — this
 *   one carries none).
 * · min-height is zeroed on the frames. The wrapper floors .plate at 180px,
 *   which would fight the design's fixed 186px rows the moment they shrink.
 * · The italic rust emphasis inside the headline comes from the WRAPPER's
 *   global `.h2 em` rule, not from here: set:html builds that <em> after
 *   scoping, so no scoped selector could reach it. Nothing in this file needs
 *   :global() precisely because the wrapper already owns it.
 * · The hover lift is the only thing here the static design file could not
 *   express. The wrapper ships `transition: transform .5s ease` on .ph, which
 *   is dead weight unless a section moves it, and a photo mosaic is where it
 *   belongs. It is a 1.04 scale inside an already-clipped plate, and
 *   reduced-motion cancels it.
 * · An apostrophe in an upload path would close the `url('…')` and blank the
 *   tile, so the path is percent-escaped FOR CSS ONLY — the raw value still
 *   travels into data-image-field, which is what the editor reads back.
 *
 * ── ACCESSIBILITY ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 * A tile is announced only when the owner gave it words. With a caption the
 * photo layer is role="img" named by THAT CAPTION — the owner's own words —
 * and the caption is also the visible pill. With no caption the layer is
 * hidden from the tree rather than announced as "image"; inventing "Cafe
 * photo" would publish a description of something nobody described. The two
 * attributes are mutually exclusive and BOTH conditional, so no literal
 * aria-hidden ever sits beside a conditional one.
 */
interface Props { content: any; layout?: any; }
const { content } = Astro.props;

const gallery = (content && content.gallery) || {};
const tag = typeof gallery.tag === 'string' ? gallery.tag.trim() : '';
const headline = typeof gallery.headline === 'string' ? gallery.headline.trim() : '';

type Tile = { image?: string; caption?: string };
// The legacy bare-array form lives at content.gallery itself — NOT the
// `gallery` alias above, which is the object form's home. Both shapes reach
// production, so both are read.
const raw: Tile[] = Array.isArray(gallery.items)
  ? gallery.items
  : (Array.isArray(content?.gallery) ? (content.gallery as Tile[]) : []);

const tiles = raw
  .map((t, i) => ({
    image: (t && typeof t.image === 'string' ? t.image : '').trim(),
    caption: (t && typeof t.caption === 'string' ? t.caption : '').trim(),
    i,
  }))
  .filter((t) => Boolean(t.image));

const n = tiles.length;

// CSS-only escape; the raw value still goes into data-image-field.
const cssUrl = (s: string) => s.replace(/'/g, '%27');

/* Row completion, per breakpoint. THREE tracks: the design's five fill rows 1–2
   exactly, so extras start a fresh three-wide row — a remainder of 1 stretches
   the last frame over all three tracks, a remainder of 2 over two. Four photos
   is its own case (the fourth takes both narrow tracks). TWO tracks: the lead
   spans both rows, tiles 2 and 3 finish the second column, and the extras run
   two per row — an odd remainder stretches the last frame across the pair. */
const rem3 = n > 5 ? (n - 5) % 3 : 0;
const fillDesk = n === 4 ? 2 : (rem3 === 1 ? 3 : (rem3 === 2 ? 2 : 0));
const fillMid = n >= 4 && (n - 3) % 2 === 1 ? 2 : 0;
---
{n > 0 && (
  <section class="gal" id="gallery">
    <div class="wrap">
      {(tag || headline) && (
        <div class="gal-head reveal">
          {tag && <span class="kick" data-field="gallery.tag">{tag}</span>}
          {headline && <h2 class="h2 gal-h2" data-field="gallery.headline" set:html={headline}></h2>}
        </div>
      )}

      <div
        class:list={['bento', 'reveal', n === 1 && 'g-1', n === 2 && 'g-2', n === 3 && 'g-3']}
        data-delay="1"
      >
        {tiles.map((t, pos) => (
          <figure
            class:list={[
              'plate',
              'frame',
              pos === n - 1 && fillDesk === 3 && 'fill-d3',
              pos === n - 1 && fillDesk === 2 && 'fill-d2',
              pos === n - 1 && fillMid === 2 && 'fill-m2',
            ]}
          >
            <div
              class="ph"
              role={t.caption ? 'img' : undefined}
              aria-label={t.caption || undefined}
              aria-hidden={t.caption ? undefined : 'true'}
              data-image-field={`gallery.items.${t.i}.image`}
              style={`background-image:url('${cssUrl(t.image)}');`}
            ></div>
            {t.caption && (
              <figcaption class="cap" data-field={`gallery.items.${t.i}.caption`}>{t.caption}</figcaption>
            )}
          </figure>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
)}

<style>
  /* The design draws this section on the cream ground with no band: its own
     92px of air above, and below it the 96px the espresso band asks for as
     `margin-top:96px`. HowBL deliberately carries no top margin, so that gap is
     carried HERE — zero this bottom and the mosaic touches the band. See the
     frontmatter. Out-specifies the wrapper's `section` rule on class count. */
  .gal { padding: clamp(56px, 7.6vw, 92px) 0 clamp(56px, 7.6vw, 96px); }

  /* The design's 34px down to the mosaic, carried by the head BLOCK so an
     eyebrow with no headline still clears the photos. See the frontmatter. */
  .gal-head { margin: 0 0 34px; }

  /* .26em above the headline, per the design; nothing below it (the block owns
     that). This is the one headline the design does not cap, so the wrapper's
     24ch is released here. */
  .gal-h2 {
    margin: .26em 0 0;
    max-width: none;
  }

  /* ── THE MOSAIC ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     The design's tracks verbatim: 2fr / 1fr / 1fr, two fixed 186px rows, 13px
     gaps, and the lead print spanning both rows. --row is a single knob so the
     breakpoints move the row height without restating the grid.

     minmax(0,…) on every track, never a bare fr: a background-image tile has no
     intrinsic width, but a caption pill does, and one long caption in a bare
     1fr track would push the whole page sideways on a phone. */
  .bento {
    --row: 186px;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 2fr) minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(0, 1fr);
    grid-template-rows: var(--row) var(--row);
    grid-auto-rows: var(--row);
    gap: 13px;
  }
  .bento .frame:first-child { grid-row: span 2; }

  /* Ground (--well), 14px radius and the clip all arrive with .plate, which is
     exactly what the design specifies per tile. Only geometry is set here. The
     wrapper floors .plate at 180px, which would fight the fixed rows, so that
     floor is released. */
  .frame {
    margin: 0;
    min-height: 0;
    min-width: 0;
  }

  /* The one thing a static design file cannot state. See the frontmatter. */
  .frame:hover .ph { transform: scale(1.04); }
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .frame:hover .ph { transform: none; }
  }

  /* ── THE COUNT LADDER. Each tier restates the tracks for its own count so no
        tier can leave a hole. Two compounds plus Astro's scope attribute, so
        they out-specify the base grid above and the collapse rules below —
        wherever a tier is already narrow enough to survive the collapse. */
  .bento.g-1 { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); }
  .bento.g-2,
  .bento.g-3 { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 2fr) minmax(0, 1fr); }
  .bento.g-2 .frame { grid-row: span 2; }

  /* Row completion on the trailing frame (see the frontmatter note). */
  .bento .frame.fill-d3 { grid-column: span 3; }
  .bento .frame.fill-d2 { grid-column: span 2; }

  /* ── 900px · the three-track bento collapses to two. The lead keeps its
        two-row span, which is what makes 3, 4 and 5 photos all land on complete
        rows here. .g-1 is deliberately NOT restated — it already out-specifies
        the base, so a lone photo keeps the full width instead of shrinking to a
        half-width tile beside an empty track — and .g-2 / .g-3 keep the
        design's lopsided 2fr / 1fr for the same reason. */
  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    .bento {
      --row: clamp(138px, 20vw, 174px);
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(0, 1fr);
      gap: 11px;
    }
    .bento .frame.fill-d3,
    .bento .frame.fill-d2 { grid-column: auto; }
    .bento .frame.fill-m2 { grid-column: span 2; }
    .gal-head { margin-bottom: 28px; }
  }

  /* ── 520px · one column. A 150px-wide tile is too small to read as a room, so
        the mosaic becomes a stack and every span is reset so nothing reaches
        for a track that is not there. Nothing may push the page sideways at
        this width: the tracks are minmax(0,…), the frames cannot exceed them,
        the wrapper already breaks a long caption inside its pill, and a pasted
        URL in the eyebrow breaks instead of scrolling the body. */
  @media (max-width: 520px) {
    .bento {
      --row: clamp(150px, 44vw, 200px);
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
      gap: 10px;
    }
    .bento.g-2,
    .bento.g-3 { grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr); }
    .bento .frame,
    .bento.g-2 .frame,
    .bento .frame:first-child { grid-row: auto; }
    .bento .frame.fill-m2 { grid-column: auto; }
    /* …except the solo photo, which stays a two-row print so it still reads as
       the lead image it is and not a stray thumbnail. Declared last. */
    .bento.g-1 .frame { grid-row: span 2; }

    .gal-head .kick {
      max-width: 100%;
      overflow-wrap: anywhere;
    }
    .gal-head { margin-bottom: 24px; }
  }
</style>
